  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 245 PM CDT Thu May 28 2026\n\nThe 19Z/2 pm surface analysis shows 1027 mb high pressure over\nLake Superior and northern MI and ridging into IL. A weak cold\nfront had pushed sw of CWA nearing the MO/IL border. Bands of\ncirrus clouds were over central and southern IL with partly to\nmostly sunny skies over CWA. East to NE winds 7-15 mph and \ngusts up to around 20 mph with warm temperatures of 83-89F. \nDewpoints were in the mid 50s to lower 60s in central IL and mid\n60s in southeast IL south of I-70. \n\nAn Omega Block pattern will prevail across the US through the\nmiddle of next week, featuring strong upper lows/trofs over the\nWestern States and New England with an upper level ridge \nsandwiched between them over the Midwest/IL. Surface high\npressure over the Western Great Lakes will shift into the\nsouthern Great Lakes Friday. Another Canadian high pressure will\nsettle southward from Hudson Bay into the Great Lakes and Ohio\nvalley this weekend and into the mid Atlantic States during\nmiddle of next week while ridging into IL. Synoptic subsidence\nfrom the surface and upper level high pressure ridges will \nkeep much of the area dry during the next 7 days. A band of\nmoisture south and west of IL may see isolated to scattered\nshowers move into sw and west central IL Fri night into Sat\nmorning with slight pops from Macomb to Jacksonville to \nEffingham sw. More mid/high clouds will spread ne into central \nIL during Friday and continue Fri night and Saturday with mostly\ncloudy skies. More cloud cover and persistent east to northeast\nflow will see a bit cooler temperatures next few days, into this\nweekend with highs in the upper 70s/lower 80s. Highs in the\nupper 70s to mid 80s are on tap from Monday through next\nThursday which is near or slightly above normal highs for early\nJune. \n\nThe Climate Prediction Center (CPC) 8-14 Day Outlook for June\n5-11th has a 50-60% chance of above normal temperatures over\ncentral and southeast IL and precipitation trending near normal.",
